WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:05.080
SQL injection can let attackers alter database queries and access, modify,

00:00:05.300 --> 00:00:09.220
or delete application data. In multi-tenant SaaS systems,

00:00:09.440 --> 00:00:13.680
injection can threaten both individual customer data and shared platform data.

00:00:13.900 --> 00:00:18.940
Command injection can allow remote command execution through diagnostics, file conversion,

00:00:19.160 --> 00:00:22.040
import jobs, or administrative utilities.

00:00:22.260 --> 00:00:26.180
This can turn a normal application feature into a platform compromise path.

00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:29.920
NoSQL and LDAP injection follow the same pattern:

00:00:30.140 --> 00:00:33.660
attacker-controlled input changes the meaning of a query or filter.

00:00:33.880 --> 00:00:39.240
Search, identity, directory, and API features need strong input boundaries.

00:00:39.460 --> 00:00:43.860
Parameterized queries, input validation, and output encoding work together.

00:00:44.080 --> 00:00:50.160
Parameterization separates data from instructions, validation rejects unexpected input,

00:00:50.380 --> 00:00:55.900
and encoding protects data when it is displayed. Use parameterized queries and safe APIs.

00:00:56.120 --> 00:00:58.920
Validate input against strict expected formats.

00:00:59.140 --> 00:01:03.140
Encode output for the correct context and avoid leaking detailed errors.

00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:09.280
Selected SOC 2 mappings for this topic include CC7.1 - Vulnerability management;

00:01:09.500 --> 00:01:12.860
CC7.2 - Monitoring for anomalies.
